'''United States 1 – 0 England''' was an [[association football]] match played on 29 June 1950, where the [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]] defeated [[England national football team|England]] in the [[1950 FIFA World Cup|World Cup]] group match at [[Arena Independência|Estádio Independência]] in [[Belo Horizonte]], Minas Gerais, Brazil.

Before the game, England were heavy favorites against a hastily assembled U.S. team, which was composed of part-time players. The game's only goal was scored by Haitian-born U.S. center forward [[Joe Gaetjens]]. This game and the U.S. team were profiled by author [[Geoffrey Douglas]] in his book ''The Game of Their Lives'', which was made into a film of the same name (later renamed ''[[The Game of Their Lives (2005 film)|The Miracle Match]]''). The match is widely considered as one of the greatest upsets in the history of the sport.

==Background==
The [[1950 FIFA World Cup]] was England's World Cup debut, [[the Football Association]] having boycotted the previous three tournaments owing to a dispute with [[FIFA]] over payments made to amateur players,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/history/newsid_1632000/1632201.stm|title=World Cup History: Uruguay 1930|date=11 April 2002|work=[[BBC Online]]|access-date=11 April 2012}}</ref> which had been resolved four years earlier.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/hi/history/newsid_1632000/1632208.stm|title=World Cup History: Brazil 1950|date=17 April 2002|work=[[BBC Online]]|access-date=11 April 2012}}</ref> England and the United States were both drawn in [[1950 FIFA World Cup#Group 2|Group 2]], along with [[Spain national football team|Spain]] and [[Chile national football team|Chile]]: under [[1950 FIFA World Cup#Format|the rules of the competition]] only the group winner from this four-nation [[Round-robin tournament|round-robin]] would progress to the final stage. Each team had played one previous match in the group, England having beaten Chile 2–0 and the United States losing 3–1 to Spain.<ref name="fifa1950">{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=7/results/index.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070624112100/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/edition=7/results/index.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=24 June 2007|title=1950 FIFA World Cup Brazil™|publisher=[[FIFA]]|access-date=11 April 2012}}</ref>

At the time, the English had a reputation as the "Kings of Football",<ref name="espnuk">{{cite web|url=http://www.espn.co.uk/football/sport/story/27321.html|title=The Miracle on Grass as USA beat England|date=9 June 2010|publisher=[[ESPN]]|access-date=16 April 2012|archive-date=13 January 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140113120026/http://www.espn.co.uk/football/sport/story/27321.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> with a [[post-war]] record of 23 wins, 4 losses, and 3 draws. They had beaten the Italians 4–0 and the Portuguese 10–0 in [[Lisbon]] two weeks before that. In comparison, the Americans, despite having reached the semi-finals of the [[1930 FIFA World Cup|inaugural 1930 World Cup]], had lost their last seven international matches (including the [[1934 World Cup]] and [[Football at the 1948 Summer Olympics|1948 Summer Olympics]]) by the combined score of 45–2, including heavy losses to Italy (7–1), Norway (11–0) and Northern Ireland (5–0). The odds were 3–1 the English would win the Cup, and 500–1 for the U.S.<ref name="Douglas1996">{{cite book | last=Douglas | first=Geoffery | title=The Game of Their Lives | isbn=0-8050-3875-2 | year=1996 | publisher=[[Henry Holt and Company]] | location=New York | url-access=registration | url=https://archive.org/details/gameoftheirlives00geof }}</ref>{{rp|5}} The people from [[Belo Horizonte]] were eager to watch the English team, with 10,000 tickets sold along with 3,000 distributed to associates of {{ill|Sete de Setembro Futebol Clube|pt}}, then-owners of the newly inaugurated [[Arena Independência|Estádio Independência]].<ref name=em>{{Cite news|author=Viana, Arnaldo|language=pt|date=7 December 2013|url=http://www.em.com.br/app/noticia/gerais/2013/12/07/interna_gerais,476957/bh-volta-a-sediar-jogos-da-copa-do-mundo.shtml|title=BH volta a sediar jogos da Copa do Mundo|work=[[Estado de Minas]]}}</ref>

England had [[Stanley Matthews]] available, who was widely considered one of the best players in the world at the time,<ref name="Douglas1996"/>{{rp|99}} but he had not played with the English team in the three international matches before the World Cup (in fact, he had joined the team late, having been touring [[Canada]] as part of another group of English internationals). As such, the selection committee (consisting entirely of [[Arthur Drewry]], then president of [[The Football League]] in England and later the [[president of FIFA]]) opted to stay with the team that had just defeated [[Chile national football team|Chile]]. Matthews would be saved for later, supposedly more difficult, opponents.<ref name="espn"/> As there were no substitutes allowed in the rules of football at the time, Matthews watched the game with the other reserves.<ref name="Lisi2011">{{cite book|last=Lisi|first=Clemente|title=A History of the World Cup: 1930–2010|url=https://archive.org/details/historyofworldcu0000lisi_v7i5|url-access=registration|access-date=16 April 2012|date=28 March 2011|publisher=Scarecrow Press|isbn=9780810877542|page=[https://archive.org/details/historyofworldcu0000lisi_v7i5/page/52 52]}}</ref> Incidentally, the England team which toured Canada beat the United States team preparing for the World Cup 1–0 at the [[Triborough Stadium]] in [[New York City]] on 19 June, 10 days before the World Cup match, courtesy of a [[Johnny Hancocks]] goal.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.11v11.com/matches/usa-v-england-19-june-1950-269777/|title=USA v England, 19 June 1950|work=11v11.com}}</ref>

The American team consisted of semi-professional players, most of whom had other jobs to support their families. [[Walter Bahr]] was a high school teacher, [[Frank Borghi]] drove a [[hearse]] for his uncle's [[funeral home]]<ref name="Douglas1996"/>{{rp|14}} and others worked as [[letter carriers]] or dishwashers.<ref>{{cite web |last=Lopez | first=Tobias | url=http://ussoccerplayers.com/exclusives/469509.html | title=Walter Bahr remembers | date=22 February 2004 | publisher=US National Soccer Team Players Association |archive-url = https://archive.today/20060529025748/http://ussoccerplayers.com/exclusives/469509.html |archive-date = 29 May 2006}}</ref> One player, [[Benny McLaughlin|Ben McLaughlin]], had to withdraw from the tournament because he could not get time off work.<ref name="espn"/> The hastily assembled team had trained together only once, which happened to be on the day before they left for Brazil, against the touring English team featuring Matthews.<ref>{{cite book | title=Soccer in a Football World: The Story of America's Forgotten Game | first=David | last=Wangerin | publisher=Temple University Press | year=2008 | isbn=978-1592138852 | page=[https://archive.org/details/soccerinfootball0000wang/page/110 110] | url=https://archive.org/details/soccerinfootball0000wang| url-access=registration}}</ref> (Matthews did not play in that game, as he was injured.<ref name="soccerhall"/>) Three players—[[Joe Maca]], [[Ed McIlvenny]], and [[Joe Gaetjens]], none of whom was a U.S. citizen—had been added to the roster just before that game.<ref name="espn">{{cite news | url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/world-cup/story/_/id/4937012/ce/us/real-story-1950-world-cup-hero?cc=5901&ver=us | title=Chasing Gaetjens | first=Leander | last=Schaerlaeckens | work=[[ESPNsoccernet]] | date=26 February 2010 | access-date=26 February 2010 | archive-date=1 March 2010 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100301191113/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/world-cup/story/_/id/4937012/ce/us/real-story-1950-world-cup-hero?cc=5901&ver=us | url-status=dead }}</ref> "We have no chance," recently appointed coach [[William Jeffrey (American soccer)|Bill Jeffrey]] told the press;<ref name="Douglas1996"/>{{rp|16}} he also declared his players "sheep ready to be slaughtered".<ref name="espn"/> The English ''[[Daily Express]]'' newspaper wrote: "It would be fair to give the U.S. three goals of a start."<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/usa/7821735/England-v-USA-Fabio-Capellos-men-need-to-fear-lesson-of-Belo-Horizonte.html|title=England v. USA: Fabio Capello's men need to fear lesson of Belo Horizonte|last=Briggs|first=Simon|date=11 June 2010|work=[[The Daily Telegraph]]|access-date=9 March 2012}}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===

====First half====
Although [[Walter Bahr]] regularly served as captain for the United States, [[Ed McIlvenny]] was selected for the role for this particular match "because he was British".<ref name="soccerhall"/> England won the toss and elected to kick off. Within ninety seconds, [[Stanley Mortensen]] sent a cross from the left wing to [[Roy Bentley]], who let off a shot that was barely pushed aside by U.S. [[Goalkeeper (association football)|goalkeeper]] Frank Borghi. By the 12th minute, England had six clear shots on goal but could not convert, with two shots hitting the post, one just going over the top, and another brilliantly saved by Borghi.<ref name="Douglas1996"/>{{rp|22–24}}

The U.S. struggled to move to the offense, and finally managed a shot on goal in the 25th minute, which was blocked by English goalkeeper [[Bert Williams (footballer, born 1920)|Bert Williams]]. The English counterattacked with three successive clear shots at the goal in minutes 30, 31, and 32, but failed to score. Mortensen twice went over the crossbar, and [[Tom Finney]]'s header to the top corner was tipped away by Borghi.<ref name="Douglas1996"/>{{rp|37–42}} In the 37th minute, Bahr took a long shot from 25 yards out, but as Williams moved to his right to intercept, Gaetjens dived headlong near the penalty spot,<ref name="nyt20091210">{{cite news| url=https://www.nytimes.com/2009/12/10/sports/soccer/10soccer.html?pagewanted=all | work=The New York Times | first=Jeré | last=Longman | title=How a 'Band of No-Hopers' Forged U.S. Soccer's Finest Day | date=10 December 2009}}</ref> and grazed the ball enough to put it to the left of the English goalkeeper, whose momentum prevented him from changing direction, and into the back of the net. The crowd exploded as the U.S. improbably led 1–0. As the half drew to a close, Finney had a chance to equalize, but the whistle blew before he could shoot.<ref name="Douglas1996" />{{rp|62}}

The crowd, initially 10,000, increased as Brazilian locals heard radio coverage of the match.<ref name="espn"/> The spectators were mostly supporting the [[underdog]] Americans, cheering with Borghi's saves and the unsuccessful English attacks.<ref name=em/> According to Bahr, "The overwhelming majority was Brazilians, but they rooted for us the entire time. We didn't realize why until after. They [were] hoping we would beat England and that Brazil would not have to play England in the final game."<ref name="espn"/> Some locals even jumped a short wall to enter the stadium [[pitch invasion|through the playing field]].<ref name="em"/>

====Second half====
The U.S. played with renewed confidence as the second half opened, creating another scoring opportunity in the 54th minute.<ref name="Douglas1996"/>{{rp|102–104}} In the 59th minute England was awarded a [[direct free kick]] but Mortensen's shot was well saved by Borghi. England began threatening again, and it was fifteen minutes before the Americans were able to get another shot. With eight minutes left, [[Charlie Colombo]] brought down Mortensen with an illegal tackle at the edge of the penalty area. England pleaded for a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty kick]], but the referee ruled it was outside the box. From [[Alf Ramsey]]'s resulting free kick, [[Jimmy Mullen (footballer, born 1923)|Jimmy Mullen]] headed the ball for what he thought was a goal, but Borghi tipped it away at the last instant, and despite the protests of the English players, the referee ruled that the ball had not crossed the line and thus there was no goal. England did not threaten again, but the U.S. had one final chance in the 85th minute: [[Frank "Peewee" Wallace]] drew Williams out of position and fired a shot at the empty net, only to be denied by a sliding goal line clearance by Ramsey.<ref name="Douglas1996"/>{{rp|131–134}}

