== Post-match ==

===Remaining matches===
Even after this loss, England could have kept their chances alive by winning their final pool match against Spain, a result which would have forced a play-off for the right to progress to the final stage of the competition.<ref name="echo"/> However, they were unable to do this, losing 1–0, and finishing the group stage with a record of 1–0–2. The U.S. also lost their next match 5–2, versus Chile, ending their 1950 World Cup run with a group stage record of 1–0–2.<ref name="fifa1950"/>  Spain advanced to the four-team [[1950 FIFA World Cup#Final round|Final Round]], finishing fourth.

===Aftermath===
[[John Souza]], the U.S. inside right forward, was selected to the World Cup All-Star team by the [[Brazil]]ian sports newspaper ''Mundo Esportivo'', and remained the only U.S. player selected to a World Cup All-Star team until [[Claudio Reyna]] in 2002.<ref name="soccerhall">{{cite web | last=Jose | first=Colin | url=http://national.soccerhall.org/ColinJose/The%20Real%20Story%20about%20the%201950%20U.S.%20World%20Cup%20Team.htm | title=The Real Story about the 1950 U.S. World Cup Team | publisher=National Soccer Hall of Fame | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100715154244/http://national.soccerhall.org/ColinJose/The%20Real%20Story%20about%20the%201950%20U.S.%20World%20Cup%20Team.htm | archive-date=15 July 2010}}</ref> Newspaper headlines in most World Cup nations trumpeted the shocking upset, except ironically in the United States and England. There was only one U.S. journalist at the World Cup: [[Dent McSkimming]] of the ''[[St. Louis Post-Dispatch]]''; he could not persuade the newspaper to pay for the trip, and had taken time off work to cover the event.<ref name="Douglas1996" />{{rp|4}} McSkimming's report of the match was one of the few to appear in a major U.S. newspaper;<ref name="Douglas1996" />{{rp|141}} <ref>{{cite journal |journal=St Louis Post-Dispatch |title=U.S. Upsets England in Soccer: Gaetjens Scores the Only Goal |date=30 June 1950 |page=3B |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/20099079/usa_1_england_0_1950/}}</ref> some other journals carried agency reports of the match.<ref>{{cite journal |journal=Pittsburgh Press |title=U.S. Scores Upset In World Soccer: Yanks Defeat England, 1–0 |date=30 June 1950 |page=30 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/20099130/yanks_defeat_england/}}</ref>

In England, newspapers at the time were generally small, and had only a few pages devoted to sports. Because the [[English cricket team]] happened to suffer their [[West Indian cricket team in England in 1950|first-ever home defeat]] to the [[West Indies cricket team|West Indies]] the very same day, that was the major story in many publications.<ref name="soccerhall" /> The team's performance was nonetheless excoriated, with headlines such as "England Caned At Soccer Too",<ref>{{cite journal| journal=Birmingham Gazette |title=England Caned at Soccer Too |date=30 June 1950 |page=5}}</ref> and "The Last Straw – U.S. Beat England In World Cup".<ref>{{cite journal |journal=Western Daily Press and Bristol Mirror |title=The Last Straw – U.S. Beat England In World Cup |date=30 June 1950 |page=5}}</ref> Many newspapers repeated Arthur Drewry's statement that the defeat was "unbelievable".<ref name="echo">{{cite journal |journal=Sunderland Echo |title=League President: "It's Unbelievable" – England's Cup Display Worst Ever |page=11 |date=30 June 1950 }}</ref>

Legend has it that in publications that did report the World Cup match, so unexpected was the result that it was presumed that the 1–0 scoreline was a typing error and so it was reported that England had won on a scoreline of 10–0 or 10–1.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport3/worldcup2002/low/team_pages/usa/newsid_1964000/1964700.stm | title=States of euphoria | work=[[BBC Online]] | first=Sean | last=Wheelock | date=8 May 2002 | access-date=13 October 2008}}</ref> However, historical newspapers online at the [[British Newspaper Archive]] show that the story is a myth.<ref name="The British Newspaper Archive">{{cite news|url=http://blog.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/2014/06/11/copies-of-old-newspapers-reveal-englands-world-cup-myth/ |title=Copies of old newspapers reveal a World Cup myth |first=Amy |last=Sell |date=11 June 2014 |work=[[British Newspaper Archive]] |access-date=11 June 2014 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140615054337/http://blog.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/2014/06/11/copies-of-old-newspapers-reveal-englands-world-cup-myth/ |archive-date=15 June 2014 }}</ref> Afterwards, a number of books and newspapers in England suggested that the U.S. team had arrived "through [[Ellis Island]]", meaning that the team was made up of imported players. In actuality, eight of the starting eleven were U.S.-born, while the other three, Gaetjens, McIlvenny, and Maca, were not U.S. citizens, but had declared their intentions to gain [[citizenship]] (only Maca ultimately became a U.S. citizen in 1957),<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.phillysoccerpage.net/2014/04/04/the-us-and-the-1950-world-cup/ | title=The US and the 1950 World Cup | first=Ed | last=Farnsworth | date=4 April 2014 | access-date=13 July 2014}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://search.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/sse.dll?rank=1&new=1&MSAV=1&msT=1&gss=angs-g&gsfn=Joseph&gsln=Maca&msbdy=1920&msddy=1982&cpxt=0&catBucket=rstp&uidh=6g6&cp=0&pcat=ROOT_CATEGORY&h=458820&recoff=6+8&db=USnaturalizationIndexes&indiv=1&ml_rpos=6 | title=United States Index of Naturalizations | year=1957 | publisher=[[Ancestry.com]]}} (subscription required)</ref> and according to the rules of the [[United States Soccer Federation]] at the time, were allowed to play. The U.S. was cleared of any wrongdoing by FIFA in a hearing on 2 December 1950.<ref name="soccerhall" /> Of the eight U.S.-born starters, five were from [[St. Louis, Missouri]].<ref name="Lange">{{cite web|url=http://www.soccermadeinstlouis.com/Soccer_Made_in_St._Louis/Extras/Entries/2011/8/5_USA_1,_England_0__The_epitaph.html|title=USA 1, England 0: The epitaph|last=Lange|first=David|date=5 August 2011|work=Soccer Made in St. Louis|access-date=16 April 2012|archive-date=18 April 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140418234121/http://www.soccermadeinstlouis.com/Soccer_Made_in_St._Louis/Extras/Entries/2011/8/5_USA_1,_England_0__The_epitaph.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

England's blue [[Kit (association football)|kit]], which had made its debut in this match, was never worn again.<ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.englandfootballonline.com/teamunif/Unif1950A.html | title= England's Blue kit 1950 | publisher=England Football Online | date=28 February 2009 | access-date=31 May 2010}}</ref> Although Walter Bahr once stated that England has never had a blue kit since then,<ref>{{Cite news | url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5hCOrbORjboCM9gcIaN5P6oAGftYw | archive-url=https://archive.today/20130124162553/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5hCOrbORjboCM9gcIaN5P6oAGftYw | url-status=dead | archive-date=24 January 2013 | title=US-England rematch evokes spirit of 1950 Cup shocker |first=Jim |last=Slater |work=Associated Press | agency=[[Agence France-Presse]] | date=26 May 2010 | access-date=12 March 2012}}</ref> the England team did wear blue in 1959 during a 1–4 away loss to [[Peru national football team|Peru]], and wore a blue strip on several occasions in the 21st century.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.goal.com/en-in/news/why-england-playing-without-names-blue-kits-switzerland/blt4a30a768eb9214ca|title=Why are England playing without names on their blue kits against Switzerland? &#124; Goal.com|website=www.goal.com}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.express.co.uk/sport/football/1415155/England-blue-kit-San-Marino-FIFA-World-Cup-2022-qualifying-match-Wembley-Stadium-blue-kit|title='Why are England playing in blue at home?' Fans left baffled at San Marino kit choice|first=Bryony|last=Smith|date=26 March 2021|website=Express.co.uk}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.irishmirror.ie/sport/soccer/soccer-news/england-wear-away-blue-kit-11478767|title=Why England are wearing blue at Wembley against Germany|first=Mick|last=Gadd|date=7 November 2017|website=Irish Mirror}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url=http://www.englandfootballonline.com/teamunif/Unif1958A.html | title= England's Away Kit 1959 | publisher=England Football Online | date=28 February 2009 | access-date=31 May 2010}}</ref> The 1950 loss was the second of several shock losses for the English team. In 1949 they lost at home to [[1949 England v Ireland football match|Ireland 0-2]], and in 1953, they were [[Match of the Century (1953 England v Hungary football match)|defeated 6–3]] by the "[[Magical Magyars]]" of Hungary and in 1954, they [[Hungary 7–1 England (1954 association football friendly)|lost again to Hungary 7–1]]. The result of these losses was a reorganization of English football that culminated in England winning the [[1966 World Cup]] on home soil.<ref name="espnuk"/>

There was little attention given to the match in the U.S. at the time, and that continued to be the case until the [[1994 World Cup]], which was hosted in the U.S.<ref name="Lange"/> [[Geoffrey Douglas]], a professor of English at the [[University of Massachusetts Amherst]], was inspired by news articles in the build-up to the 1994 tournament, and wrote a book about the 1950 match, having interviewed the five surviving members of the U.S. team. Entitled ''The Game of Their Lives'', it was made into a 2005 film of the same name directed by [[David Anspaugh]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.hollywoodscript.com/GameOfTheirLives.html|title=Interview with author Geoffrey Douglas|last=Kellem|first=Craig|publisher=HollywoodScript.com|year=2003|access-date=16 April 2012|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120404164050/http://www.hollywoodscript.com/GameOfTheirLives.html|archive-date=4 April 2012|url-status=dead|df=dmy-all}}</ref> Since then, the game has become known as "The Miracle Match",<ref name="soccerdigest">{{cite journal | url=http://findarticles.com/p/articles/mi_m0FCN/is_3_23/ai_63917331/ | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20050320142355/http://www.findarticles.com/p/articles/mi_m0FCN/is_3_23/ai_63917331 | url-status=dead | archive-date=2005-03-20 | title=Miracle on Grass | journal=Soccer Digest | first=Michael | last=Lewis | date=August 2000 | access-date=24 June 2009 }}</ref> in reference to the "[[Miracle on Ice]]" from the [[1980 Winter Olympics]], in which the [[United States men's national ice hockey team|U.S. ice hockey team]] defeated the heavily favored [[Soviet Union national ice hockey team|Soviet Union]].<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/england-v-usa-834996.html?ino=1 | title=England v. USA | work=[[The Independent]] | access-date=24 June 2009 | location=London | date=27 May 2008 | url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080601000444/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/england-v-usa-834996.html | archive-date=1 June 2008}}</ref><ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052970204621904574246780066743714 | title=Beating Spain Would Be Another U.S. 'Miracle' | work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] | first=David | last=Biderman | date=24 June 2009 | access-date=24 June 2009}}</ref> The United States and England have played two World Cup matches against each other since 1950: a match in the group stage of the [[2010 FIFA World Cup]], which ended in a 1–1 draw, and another group stage match in the [[2022 FIFA World Cup]], which ended drawn 0–0.<ref name="bbc20100602">{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/10200154|title=US v. England match recalls 1950 upset|first=Kevin|last=Connolly|date=2 June 2010|work=[[BBC Online]]|access-date=9 March 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/southafrica2010/matches/groupstage.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101208173657/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/archive/southafrica2010/matches/groupstage.html|url-status=dead|archive-date=8 December 2010|title=Group Stage Matches|work=2010 FIFA World Cup South Africa|publisher=[[FIFA]]|access-date=11 April 2012}}</ref>

